<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rhys ap Tewdwr</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1093">1093</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rhys ap Tewdwr was king of Deheubarth in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> from about 1078 to 1093. He held his throne against rivals partly through an arrangement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, who came to St Davids in 1081. In 1093 he was killed near Brecon fighting the Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-de-neufmarche">Bernard de Neufmarché</a>, and his death opened south Wales to Norman conquest. His children Gruffydd and Nest carried on his line.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhys-ap-tewdwr.jpg" alt="Rhys ap Tewdwr" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Owj20 at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medieval_Wales.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rhys ap Tewdwr was born in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthenshire">Carmarthenshire</a>, a member of the House of Dinefwr, a branch descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodri-the-great">Rhodri the Great</a>, and a great-grandson of Einion ab Owain and so a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hywel-dda">Hywel Dda</a>. He claimed the throne of Deheubarth after his second cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhys-ab-owain">Rhys ab Owain</a> was beheaded following the battle of Gwdig, at modern Goodwick, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caradog-ap-gruffydd">Caradog ap Gruffydd</a> in 1078. He married more than once; his last wife was Gwladys ferch Rhiwallon, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhiwallon-ap-cynfyn">Rhiwallon ap Cynfyn</a> of the Mathrafal dynasty of Powys.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1081 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caradog-ap-gruffydd">Caradog ap Gruffydd</a> invaded Deheubarth and Rhys took sanctuary in the Monastery of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>. Allied with Gruffudd ap Cynan, who was seeking to regain Gwynedd, he defeated and killed Caradog and his allies Trahaearn ap Caradog and Meilyr ap Rhiwallon at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mynydd-carn">Battle of Mynydd Carn</a> the same year. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>'s visit, Rhys paid him £40 a year for his kingdom, which kept relations good for the rest of William's life.</p><p>In 1088 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadwgan-ap-bleddyn">Cadwgan ap Bleddyn</a> of Powys drove him to Ireland, but Rhys returned the same year with a fleet from Ireland and defeated the men of Powys, killing two of Cadwgan's brothers, Madog and Rhiryd. When Cedifor ap Gollwyn, who traced his descent from the old kings of Dyfed, died in 1091, his sons demanded that Rhys give up the throne to Gruffudd ap Maredudd; Rhys defeated the rebels at St. Dogmaels and killed Gruffudd.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Welsh chronicles record that Rhys was slain by the Frenchmen settled in Brycheiniog, and the Brut y Tywysogion adds that with him fell the kingdom of the Britons. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> then seized much of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and fought the chieftains of Powys and Gwynedd over it. His eldest son Gruffydd was later allowed a small part of the kingdom, and through him Rhys was an ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-tudor">Tudor dynasty</a>. His daughter Nest bore a son to Henry I and then married Gerald de Windsor, Constable of Pembroke; their sons and grandsons, the FitzGerald conquerors of Ireland, were known as the sons of Nest.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rhys ap Tewdwr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1093 the Norman Bernard de Neufmarché defeated and killed <b>Rhys ap Tewdwr</b> near Brecon and created the Norman lordship of Brecknock, which later became the county of Brecknockshire.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-brycheiniog">Kingdom of Brycheiniog</a></li><li><blockquote>Bernard de Neufmarche invaded the kingdom of Brycheiniog in the late 11th century and killed <b>Rhys ap Tewdwr</b> of the Kingdom of Deheubarth near Brecon in 1093.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-brecknock">Lordship of Brecknock</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthenshire">Carmarthenshire</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Brecon, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.86,-4.31&amp;year=1083" aria-label="Carmarthenshire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Rhys ap Tewdwr"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4773 5687h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4773 5687h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.86,-4.31&amp;year=1083">What was here in 1083? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
